</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(7)$"><p>
The counterexamples described in this section have one feature in common: the underlying space $X$ is infinite-dimensional. In fact, this is necessary: if the space $X$ is finite-dimensional (in a suitable sense), then the $\infty $-topos $\Shv (X)$ is hypercomplete (Corollary <a href="/tag/877955" data-tag="877955">7.2.1.12</a>). This finite-dimensionality condition on $X$ is satisfied in many of the situations to which the theory of simplicial presheaves is commonly applied, such as the Nisnevich topology on a scheme of finite Krull dimension. 
